{
  "result": "There is a specific kind of light that only exists at 5:42 a.m. in October. It's not quite sunrise, not quite night, and it makes everything look like a memory. \n\nI think about this light a lot, especially when I'm stuck in fluorescent rooms that try so hard to pretend time doesn't pass. We spend so much of our lives trying to smooth out the edges of time \u2014 with coffee, with deadlines, with scrolling \u2014 but time doesn't want to be smoothed. It wants to be felt. \n\nMy grandmother kept a drawer full of useless things. A single earring, a ticket stub from 1978, a smooth stone from a beach she couldn't remember the name of, a dozen keys that opened nothing. I used to think it was clutter. Now I think it was a museum. Not of important events, but of the feeling of having lived. She understood that a life isn't really measured in milestones, but in the quiet accumulation of small, strange evidence that you were here. \n\nWe are all curators of invisible museums. The song you play when you're sad but don't want to cry. The way you still set an extra plate out of habit. The joke you repeat even though no one laughs anymore. \n\nMaybe meaning isn't something you find. Maybe it's something you collect, one small, useless, beautiful thing at a time, until suddenly you have enough to call it a life. And that, I think, is enough. More than enough, honestly speaking.",
